# Two throws into the pile of 300: hundreds of bodies awake at once, far more than one state
# packet holds. stale_ticks.max is the sender's own promise, not a sync check: it holds with no
# network at all. Its bound is max(4, 30 + ceil(M / N)) ticks; here M is about 300 awake bodies
# and N at least 55 records of a 1000-byte packet (a copy with every rare group), so 36, read
# as 40 with a margin. The stub sends everything every tick and reads 0.
scenario load_overflow
scene load300
seed 31
guests 1
settle 10s

net g1 rtt 80ms jitter 10ms loss 2%

p0 walk load200
p0 grab load200 as a0
p0 wait 300ms
p0 throw 0 0.5 0 speed 12
p1 walk load299
p1 grab load299 as a1
p1 wait 300ms
p1 throw 0 0.5 0 speed 12

expect result a0 granted
expect result a1 granted
expect stale_ticks.max <= 40t
expect agree_at_rest 5cm
expect no_stuck
# A body the budget starved plays linearly across the ticks it missed; where that gap hid an
# impact the copy shows a step there, one per hit body. step.count <= 16 is measured (8 to 12
# seen), twice that with a margin, not derived. A
# body that waited and then gets a snapshot many ticks ahead past the seam's speed is placed
# there and the render offset takes the move: no drawn move past its decay of 0.15 x 2 m a
# tick plus a 12 m/s throw's 0.2 m: jump.max is derived.
expect step.count <= 16
expect jump.max <= 0.5m
# behind_ticks: a copy plays at most delay_max (15 ticks, before the clock knows the path)
# behind its owner's newest, plus the one-way delay and the jitter in ticks, plus 2 for the
# tick the packet arrives in and the tick the owner's newest is read after its step:
# rtt 80 ms (3), 10 ms jitter (1): 21. Derived, on the p95: the stub's naive sender saturates
# the path here and its queue adds to the one-way delay (28 ticks at worst).
expect behind_ticks.p95 <= 21t
